Validation / Testing

The monitoring pipeline was tested by:

Uploading images to the S3 source bucket.
Verifying Lambda execution.
Confirming successful PNG output in the processed S3 bucket.
Verifying CloudWatch Logs.
Confirming custom metric values.
Triggering Lambda alarms using configured thresholds.
Verifying SNS email notifications.
Confirming metrics populate correctly in the CloudWatch Dashboard.
Testing Lambda timeout behavior to validate the custom timeout metric.


Key Lessons Learned :

AWS Lambda automatically provides several built-in CloudWatch metrics; they do not need to be created manually.
Custom metrics can be created from meaningful application log events using CloudWatch Log Metric Filters.
A metric filter processes new log events; it does not retroactively process old logs.
value = "1" causes each matching log event to contribute one metric unit.
CloudWatch alarm thresholds depend on the metric's period, statistic, and evaluation periods.
Lambda throttling is different from invocation count; a high invocation count does not automatically mean throttling.
Lambda account concurrency limits can prevent reserved concurrency from being configured.
Terraform cannot directly access resources inside a child module without an output exposing the required value.
CloudWatch Dashboard metric arrays are positional:
[ Namespace, MetricName ]
Existing AWS resources can be brought under Terraform management using terraform import.
CloudWatch dashboards provide a single operational view of application health by combining built-in and custom metrics.

Project Outcome

The project demonstrates a complete serverless monitoring workflow:

S3 → Lambda → CloudWatch → Alarms → SNS → Email

with both infrastructure monitoring and application-level monitoring implemented using Terraform.